(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-pd1-exam2-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Blood pressure should not be assessed on an arm with an IV line, an AV (atrioventricular) fistula for hemodialysis, a brachial artery bruit, or...","choices":["there are no contraindicated sites for BP (blood pressure) cuff placement","a radical mastectomy/lymph node dissection on that side, or lymphedema","a normal, healthy arm with no history","an arm with well-manicured nails"],"answer":1,"exp":"BP (blood pressure) should be avoided on an arm with an IV line, AV (atrioventricular) fistula, brachial artery bruit, prior radical mastectomy/lymph node dissection on that side, lymphedema, thrombophlebitis, or recent fracture/trauma, all of which can either be harmed by cuff pressure or produce an inaccurate reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"Epitrochlear lymph nodes are palpated in which location?","choices":["Along the anterior wrist crease, just proximal to the thenar eminence, with the wrist held in slight extension","In the groove between the biceps and triceps muscles, about 3 cm above the medial epicondyle, with the elbow flexed ~90 degrees","Directly over the antecubital crease, medial to the biceps tendon, with the elbow fully extended and supinated","In the axilla with the arm fully abducted, pressing the fingertips up and in toward the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct technique and location for palpating epitrochlear nodes. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"During the posterior thyroid exam, what maneuver helps the examiner feel the isthmus?","choices":["Ask the patient to hold their breath while pressing above the thyroid cartilage","Place fingers below the cricoid cartilage and ask the patient to swallow","Have the patient hyperextend the neck while the examiner auscultates","No specific maneuver is needed; the isthmus is always easily palpable at rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Placing fingers below the cricoid cartilage while the patient swallows allows the examiner to feel the isthmus move under the fingers. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Documenting current medications (for example, anticoagulants) is relevant before the anorectal exam mainly because...","choices":["medications never meaningfully affect any physical exam findings whatsoever at all","documenting it completely and permanently replaces the ongoing need for the physical exam","anticoagulant use increases bleeding risk and can affect interpretation of minor bleeding on exam","it is only relevant to the entirely separate cardiac history and physical exam findings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Patients on anticoagulants (or antiplatelet agents) have an increased bleeding tendency, which is important both for safety during the exam and for correctly interpreting any minor bleeding noted, since it may be exaggerated by the medication rather than reflecting more significant pathology. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A thorough male GU (genitourinary) history establishes context that helps the examiner...","choices":["skip the physical exam entirely whenever the patient's history seems reassuring or unremarkable","avoid the separate legal requirement of obtaining patient consent to the examination itself","focus the subsequent physical exam on the most relevant findings and differential considerations","is entirely irrelevant to any of the subsequent physical exam findings that are gathered"],"answer":2,"exp":"A focused history (urinary symptoms, discharge, pain, sexual function) helps the examiner anticipate what to look for on exam and narrows the relevant differential before physical examination even begins. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Regarding kidney palpability on exam, a key teaching point is that...","choices":["kidneys are generally non-palpable, though the right kidney (being lower than the left) may be felt in a thin patient","both kidneys are easily and reliably palpable in essentially all adult patients, regardless of build","the left kidney, which is said to sit lower than the right, is more often palpable in a thin, cooperative patient","kidney palpation requires no particular technique at all and can be performed casually by any examiner"],"answer":0,"exp":"Kidneys are generally not palpable on exam; however, because the right kidney sits somewhat lower than the left (due to the liver above it), it may occasionally be felt in a thin patient using proper technique. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 47"},{"q":"Direct versus indirect percussion, as distinguished in this lecture, differ primarily in that indirect percussion...","choices":["requires no side-to-side comparison, relying instead on a single strike per examined location","uses a pleximeter finger (placed on the chest) struck by the plexor finger of the other hand","involves striking the chest wall directly with one finger, without using a pleximeter at all","is performed only over the abdomen and is never used during the standard pulmonary exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Indirect percussion, the standard technique used clinically, involves placing the pleximeter (a finger of the non-dominant hand) firmly against the chest wall and striking it with the plexor (a finger of the dominant hand), in contrast to direct percussion, which strikes the chest wall directly. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"When inspecting and palpating the proximal and lateral nail folds, the examiner screens for redness, swelling, pain, exudate, and...","choices":["active bowel sounds present","warts, cysts, or tumors","new-onset heart murmurs","elevated blood pressure reading"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nail fold assessment specifically screens for redness, swelling, pain, exudate, and growths such as warts, cysts, or tumors. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"During extraocular movement testing, the examiner observes for any deviation of gaze, nystagmus, and...","choices":["hearing thresholds","lid lag","pupil color","visual acuity numbers"],"answer":1,"exp":"In addition to gaze deviation and nystagmus, the examiner watches for lid lag (a delay of the upper eyelid following the eye's movement) during extraocular movement testing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"To estimate the systolic pressure before formal auscultatory BP (blood pressure) assessment, the examiner palpates the radial artery, inflates the cuff until the pulse disappears, notes that pressure, and then...","choices":["uses that exact palpated reading, with no adjustment at all, as the actual cuff inflation target","disregards the palpated reading entirely and simply inflates the cuff to a fixed 200 mmHg","subtracts 30 mmHg from that reading to determine the target cuff inflation value used","adds 30 mmHg to that reading to determine the target inflation value for the actual BP (blood pressure) assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adding 30 mmHg to the palpated pulse-obliteration pressure gives a target inflation value that ensures the cuff is inflated well above the true systolic pressure during the actual auscultatory measurement, helping avoid missing the systolic reading due to an auscultatory gap. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"Are epitrochlear nodes typically palpable in healthy individuals?","choices":["Yes, but only in pediatric patients","Only palpable in patients with high BMI (body mass index)","No, they are usually not palpable in healthy individuals","Yes, they are always palpable and up to 3 cm is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture states epitrochlear nodes are usually not palpable in healthy people. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"How many groups of cervical lymph nodes are described in the lecture?","choices":["5 groups","15 groups","10 groups","3 groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists 10 cervical lymph node groups, including occipital, pre-auricular, posterior auricular, tonsillar, submandibular, submental, superficial cervical, posterior cervical, deep cervical, and supraclavicular. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 64"},{"q":"Evaluating for rectal prolapse, a special finding sometimes assessed during this exam, is best done by...","choices":["measuring the patient's seated blood pressure carefully before the maneuver begins","asking the patient to bear down/strain and observing for protrusion of rectal tissue","auscultating the abdomen carefully across all four quadrants beforehand","carefully palpating the thyroid gland for size, symmetry, and nodularity beforehand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rectal prolapse (full-thickness protrusion of the rectum through the anus) is best elicited by asking the patient to bear down or strain, which increases intra-abdominal pressure and can reveal the prolapse if present. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"An enlarged, tender prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination), especially with systemic symptoms like fever, is most consistent with...","choices":["testicular torsion","acute prostatitis","a normal prostate exam finding","a hydrocele"],"answer":1,"exp":"An acutely tender, often boggy/enlarged prostate accompanied by systemic symptoms like fever is classic for acute prostatitis, an infectious/inflammatory process, in contrast to the typically non-tender enlargement of BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia) or the hard nodularity of malignancy. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"The bimanual kidney 'capture' technique involves pushing the left hand upward under the flank while, at the peak of inspiration, the right hand...","choices":["presses firmly and deeply downward to trap the kidney between both hands","percusses firmly over the kidney region instead of applying pressure","auscultates carefully for renal artery bruits at that moment","is removed from contact with the abdomen entirely at that point"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the peak of inspiration (when the kidney has descended as far as it will), the right hand presses firmly and deeply downward, attempting to 'capture' the kidney between the two hands before it is released as the patient exhales. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"Screening tests referenced in the past medical history section for pulmonary conditions include skin testing, chest X-ray, and...","choices":["a resting echocardiogram","peripheral visual field testing","pulmonary function tests (PFTs)","a screening colonoscopy exam"],"answer":2,"exp":"The past medical history/testing section references skin testing (for example, for TB (tuberculosis)), CXR (chest x-ray), and pulmonary function tests (PFTs) as relevant prior workup to ask about. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Equipment useful for the skin exam includes a light source, ruler, and optionally a magnifying lens or...","choices":["an otoscope","a stethoscope exclusively","no equipment is ever needed","dermoscope/dermatoscope"],"answer":3,"exp":"A dermoscope (or dermatoscope), which provides magnification with cross-polarized or unpolarized light, is an optional but increasingly common piece of equipment for the skin exam, alongside a light source, ruler, and magnifying lens. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"During convergence testing, as the examiner's finger moves inward toward the bridge of the nose, the eyes should normally follow the finger until it is within approximately...","choices":["0 cm (touching the nose) in every normal patient","5 to 8 cm of the nose","50 to 80 cm of the nose","there is no normal range for convergence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal convergence allows the eyes to track the approaching finger until it comes within approximately 5-8 cm of the nose before convergence typically breaks down. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"The auscultatory gap, a period of silence between the systolic and diastolic Korotkoff sounds, can cause errors including...","choices":["having no measurable effect on blood pressure readings at all, in any patient","overestimating both the systolic and diastolic pressures by an equal amount","underestimating the true systolic pressure and overestimating the true diastolic pressure","only affecting the measured heart rate, and never the blood pressure reading itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"If unrecognized, the auscultatory gap can cause the examiner to record a systolic pressure lower than the true value (if the cuff wasn't inflated high enough to hear the first Korotkoff sound before the gap) and a diastolic pressure higher than the true value, which is why the palpated-pressure-plus-30-mmHg technique is used to avoid this error. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Pulsus parvus, referring to weak pulses, is classically seen in which condition?","choices":["Severe, longstanding aortic regurgitation","Normal healthy aging, without disease","Atherosclerotic peripheral vascular disease","Anxiety-induced sinus tachycardia alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulsus parvus (weak pulses) is classically associated with atherosclerotic PVD (peripheral vascular disease). — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"What technique should be used to palpate cervical lymph nodes?","choices":["Use the whole flat palm pressed firmly against the side of the neck","Use the thumb pressed firmly along a single straight line down the neck","Percuss the neck directly using a standard neurologic reflex hammer","Use the pads of the index and middle fingers in gentle circular motions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cervical node palpation uses the finger pads (index and middle) in gentle circles. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 65"},{"q":"Ensuring the patient is comfortable and adequately draped before beginning the anorectal exam should occur...","choices":["it is not a relevant consideration for this exam","only if the patient specifically requests it","only after the DRE (digital rectal examination) is already complete","before the exam begins, as part of preparing the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Patient comfort and appropriate draping should be established before starting the exam, as part of the preparatory steps alongside positioning and explaining the exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Because prostate cancer risk and screening recommendations can vary by individual factors, a thorough approach to counseling should account for...","choices":["only the patient's age, deliberately ignoring both race and family history factors","a strictly identical screening recommendation given to every single patient seen","the patient's age, race, and family history alongside a discussion of screening benefits/risks","ignoring family history entirely, while weighing only the patient's age and race"],"answer":2,"exp":"Individualized counseling considers age, race (for example, higher incidence in African American men), and family history alongside a balanced discussion of the benefits and limitations of screening tools like PSA (prostate-specific antigen), rather than a one-size-fits-all recommendation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness is assessed by...","choices":["using only light superficial palpation over the flank region, without ever incorporating any percussive component","placing a palm against the costovertebral angle and delivering a sharp blow to that hand with a fist, then asking about tenderness","applying deep bimanual palpation with both hands simultaneously pressed firmly against both flanks at the same time","auscultating with the stethoscope diaphragm placed directly over the costovertebral angle region on each side"],"answer":1,"exp":"With the patient sitting and the examiner standing behind, the examiner places a palm flat against the costovertebral angle and strikes that hand with the other fist (a fist percussion technique), then asks the patient about any resulting tenderness — repeated on both sides. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"Among the normal findings summarized for inspection of the chest are symmetry and...","choices":["absence of retraction or accessory muscle use","marked asymmetry of the chest wall on inspection","visible intercostal and suprasternal retractions","prominent use of accessory muscles at rest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal inspection findings include chest wall symmetry and the absence of retractions or accessory muscle use during breathing, both of which would suggest increased work of breathing if present. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Regarding glove use during the skin exam, the general practice is that gloves are...","choices":["only ever used specifically during hair and scalp inspection","never used under any circumstance during the entire skin exam","required for every single skin exam performed, without any exception","generally not used except for palpation of wounds/open lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gloves are generally not required for a routine skin exam but should be used when palpating wounds or open lesions, consistent with standard precautions for potential body fluid contact. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When using the ophthalmoscope, the correct hand/eye pairing is to...","choices":["always use the left hand and left eye, regardless of which of the patient's eyes is being examined at that moment","use whichever hand and eye combination feels most comfortable, since it makes no clinical difference at all","hold the scope in the right hand and use the right eye to examine the patient's right eye (and left hand/left eye for the patient's left eye)","always use the right hand and right eye, regardless of which of the patient's eyes is currently being examined"],"answer":2,"exp":"Proper ophthalmoscope technique matches the examiner's hand and eye to the same side as the patient's eye being examined — right hand/right eye for the patient's right eye, left hand/left eye for the patient's left eye — which avoids bumping noses and allows a natural viewing angle. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"During blood pressure auscultation, the first Korotkoff sound heard marks the systolic pressure, while the disappearance of sound marks...","choices":["nothing of clinical significance","the diastolic pressure","a second systolic reading","the pulse pressure directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"As the cuff slowly deflates, the first Korotkoff sound heard marks the systolic pressure, and the point where the sound disappears marks the diastolic pressure. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 56"},{"q":"Pulsus tardus, referring to sluggish pulses, typically occurs in which settings?","choices":["Fever with a hyperdynamic circulation","Hyperthyroidism with a hyperkinetic pulse","Aortic stenosis or low cardiac output","Isolated dehydration without heart disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulsus tardus (a slow-rising, sluggish pulse) is classically seen with aortic stenosis or states of low cardiac output. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"When a lymph node is palpated, what maneuver helps differentiate it from a muscle or artery?","choices":["Apply ice to the area and observe the response","Auscultate over the node with a stethoscope","Ask the patient to cough forcefully","Roll the node up-down and side-to-side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rolling the node in multiple directions (up-down, side-to-side) under the fingers helps distinguish a discrete lymph node from a muscle band or pulsatile artery. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 66"},{"q":"Before the anorectal exam begins, explaining the steps of the exam to the patient primarily serves to...","choices":["reduce patient anxiety and improve cooperation, given the sensitive nature of the exam","completely and permanently replace the ongoing need for correct patient positioning beforehand","entirely and permanently eliminate the separate need for appropriate patient draping throughout","speed up the overall exam considerably by skipping the initial visual inspection step"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because the anorectal exam is a sensitive, potentially anxiety-provoking exam, clearly explaining each step beforehand helps the patient feel informed and reduces apprehension, improving cooperation. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Urinary incontinence, one of the screened history items, refers to...","choices":["complete inability to urinate at all","excessive thirst","involuntary loss of urine","blood in the urine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Urinary incontinence refers to the involuntary loss of urine, an important symptom to screen for as part of the lower urinary tract/GU (genitourinary) history. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"The bladder is generally examinable on physical exam only when...","choices":["never, regardless of its degree of fullness","only while the patient remains standing upright","it remains completely empty and non-distended","it is distended above the symphysis pubis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bladder is usually not palpable/percussible unless it is distended, at which point it rises above the symphysis pubis and can be assessed for a round, smooth dome, tenderness, and the height it reaches (via percussion for dullness). — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"Regarding lobar anatomy, the right lung is divided into upper, middle, and lower lobes, while the left lung has only...","choices":["upper, middle, and lower lobes, identical to the right lung's three lobes","upper and lower lobes (with the lingular segment of the upper lobe)","a single undivided lobe, with no further lobar division present at all","four separate lobes, one more than the right lung's three lobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unlike the right lung's three lobes, the left lung has only upper and lower lobes; the lingular segment of the left upper lobe is analogous in position to the right middle lobe. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Before beginning the skin exam, the patient should be undressed and covered with a gown open in the back, and should also remove...","choices":["only their shoes","nothing; cosmetics do not need to be removed","makeup, nail polish, or other cosmetics","only jewelry, but makeup and nail polish may remain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Patient preparation includes removing makeup, nail polish, or other cosmetics that could obscure or mimic skin/nail findings, along with being gowned appropriately and considering a chaperone. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Before beginning the ophthalmoscopic exam, the examiner should darken the room, adjust the scope's light size/brightness, and turn the diopter dial to...","choices":["+20 diopters","0 diopter","there is no starting diopter setting","-20 diopters"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ophthalmoscope is initially set to 0 diopter before beginning the exam; if the examiner wears glasses, they should remove them and adjust the diopter setting to accommodate their own vision instead. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"According to the BMI (body mass index) classification table, a BMI (body mass index) of 18.5-24.9 kg/m2 is classified as...","choices":["Underweight","Normal","Obesity Class I","Overweight"],"answer":1,"exp":"A BMI (body mass index) of 18.5-24.9 kg/m2 is classified as normal weight; under 18.5 is underweight, 25.0-29.9 is overweight, and 30 or above is classified into obesity classes I, II, and III (extreme obesity, >40). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which great vessel is the largest blood vessel in the human body, originating from the left ventricle and arching to the level of the sternal angle?","choices":["Aorta","Superior vena cava","Pulmonary artery","Inferior vena cava"],"answer":0,"exp":"The aorta is the body's largest vessel, originating from the LV (left ventricle) and arching at the sternal angle before descending posteriorly and to the left. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"During scalp inspection, which findings should be noted, similar to the skin exam?","choices":["Only skull circumference","Only hair color","Only sensation to light touch","Lesions and dandruff"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scalp is inspected for lesions and dandruff, similar to a general skin exam. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Adequate lubrication of the gloved finger before the DRE (digital rectal examination) is important chiefly to...","choices":["eliminate the ongoing need for the patient to squeeze the anal sphincter","chemically disinfect and sterilize the anal canal before finger insertion","minimize patient discomfort and reduce the risk of mucosal trauma during insertion","substantially improve the examiner's ability to detect subtle rectal masses"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anal canal and rectal mucosa are sensitive tissue; adequate lubrication reduces friction, minimizing patient discomfort and the risk of causing mucosal trauma during finger insertion. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"If a patient refuses the genital examination, the examiner's most appropriate next step is to...","choices":["perform the exam despite the refusal, since it is clearly medically indicated","end the entire clinical encounter immediately and reschedule for later","document the refusal briefly and move on without further discussion","discuss the patient's reasons for refusal respectfully, without forcing the exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Respecting patient autonomy means discussing the reasons for refusal and addressing concerns where possible, without proceeding with the exam against the patient's wishes. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"A distended bladder, when examined, should have a dome that feels...","choices":["irregular and nodular","stony-hard","it cannot be assessed by touch at all","round and smooth"],"answer":3,"exp":"A distended bladder's dome should feel round and smooth on palpation; the examiner also checks for tenderness and percusses to assess how high it rises above the symphysis pubis (as an area of dullness). — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"The minor (horizontal) fissure specifically divides which lobes?","choices":["the right middle and lower lobes","the left upper and lower lobes","there is no minor fissure in normal anatomy","the right upper and middle lobes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The minor (horizontal) fissure divides the right upper lobe from the right middle lobe; the major (oblique) fissures separate the upper/middle lobes from the lower lobes on each side. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The recommended patient positioning for the skin exam in this Physical Diagnosis I course is...","choices":["supine/prone exclusively, as taught in this course","lithotomy position","Trendelenburg position","seated/standing"],"answer":3,"exp":"While supine/prone positioning is preferred by some providers, the seated/standing position is specifically the method used and taught in this Physical Diagnosis I course. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"To begin the ophthalmoscopic exam, the patient looks up over the examiner's shoulder while the examiner starts approximately how far away, at what angle?","choices":["1 inch away, directly in front of the patient's line of vision","15 inches away, at a 20-degree angle lateral to the patient's line of vision","5 feet away, standing directly behind the patient's shoulder","There is no recommended starting distance or angle to use"],"answer":1,"exp":"The examiner starts approximately 15 inches away from the patient, positioned at about a 20-degree angle lateral to the patient's line of vision, shining the light onto the pupil to note the red reflex before moving closer. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"According to the BMI (body mass index) classification table, 'Extreme obesity' (Obesity Class III) corresponds to a BMI (body mass index) of...","choices":["greater than 40 kg/m2","18.5 to 24.9 kg/m2","25.0 to 29.9 kg/m2","less than 18.5 kg/m2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extreme obesity (Obesity Class III) is classified as BMI (body mass index) greater than 40 kg/m2, the highest category on the classification table. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"The superior and inferior venae cavae drain venous blood from the body into which chamber?","choices":["Aorta","Left atrium","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both venae cavae empty venous blood into the right atrium along its medial border. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"During hair inspection, which of the following should be assessed?","choices":["Only the overall length of the scalp hair","Only the surface temperature of the scalp","Color, distribution, texture, hair loss, and nits","Only the presence of gray or white hair"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific hair inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"A rectal malignancy detected on DRE (digital rectal examination) would most classically feel like...","choices":["a normal, expected finding present in the majority of healthy adults","a soft, mobile, symmetric swelling identical in texture to surrounding tissue","something detectable only through abdominal auscultation, never by palpation","a firm, fixed, irregular mass distinct from the smooth surrounding mucosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"A rectal malignancy classically presents as a firm, often fixed (non-mobile), irregular mass that feels distinctly different from the smooth, pliable surrounding rectal mucosa. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Palpating the epididymis as a distinct step from palpating the testis itself is important because the epididymis is normally...","choices":["located anterior to the testis and fused closely enough that it cannot be palpated separately","not a normal structure, but rather always a sign of chronic infection in healthy men","identical in location and consistency to the testis, requiring no separate assessment on exam","a separate, comma-shaped structure posterior to the testis, distinguishable on exam from the testis itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"The epididymis is a distinct, comma-shaped structure that sits posterior to the testis; palpating it separately allows the examiner to distinguish epididymal findings (often more likely benign/infectious, for example, epididymitis or a spermatocele) from testicular findings (which carry more concern for malignancy when nodular). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"To assess the aortic pulsation, the examiner presses firmly deep in the upper abdomen...","choices":["far laterally in the right upper quadrant","only within the pelvic cavity itself","only directly along the costal margins","slightly to the left of the midline"],"answer":3,"exp":"The aorta is identified by pressing firmly and deeply in the upper abdomen, slightly to the left of the midline, where its pulsation can typically be felt. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Comparing the right and left main-stem bronchi, the right main-stem bronchus is characteristically...","choices":["identical in length, width, and angle to the left bronchus","shorter, wider, and more straight (vertical) than the left","longer, narrower, and more angled (horizontal) than the right","absent entirely, with only a single shared main bronchus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right main-stem bronchus is shorter, wider, and takes a more straight/vertical course compared with the left, which is why aspirated foreign material more often lodges in the right lung. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"When examining the hair and scalp, the examiner begins by standing in front of the patient and...","choices":["only ever inspecting the scalp from directly behind the seated patient","separating the hair with fingers or a cotton-tipped applicator to visualize the scalp","immediately cutting a small hair sample for later microscopic laboratory analysis","skipping direct scalp visualization entirely and relying on palpation alone instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Scalp examination begins with the examiner standing in front of the patient and using fingers or a cotton-tipped applicator to part/separate the hair, allowing direct visualization of the scalp for lesions, dandruff, nits, and so on — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"When beginning the ophthalmoscopic exam from 15 inches away, the examiner shines the light onto the pupil and notes the...","choices":["visual acuity number","nothing in particular at this step","red reflex","pupil color exclusively, unrelated to any reflex"],"answer":2,"exp":"From the initial starting position, the examiner shines the light onto the pupil and notes the red reflex — a reflection off the vascular retina that should appear as a consistent reddish-orange glow; its absence or asymmetry can be an important abnormal finding. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"When measuring weight as part of the general survey, proper technique includes having the patient remove shoes/heavy clothing, zeroing/taring the scale, and ensuring the patient is...","choices":["not supported by a wall or holding objects such as a cane","wearing their heaviest coat and boots for extra warmth","leaning firmly against the wall for added stability","holding onto a cane or handrail for balance during weighing"],"answer":0,"exp":"For an accurate weight measurement, the patient should stand unsupported (not leaning on a wall) and without holding assistive objects like a cane, in addition to removing shoes and heavy clothing and using a zeroed/tared scale. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"The mediastinum, the central compartment of the thoracic cavity housing the heart and great vessels, is bounded anteriorly by which structure?","choices":["Clavicles","Diaphragm","Vertebral bodies","Sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mediastinum is bounded by the sternum anteriorly and the vertebral bodies posteriorly. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the recommended general approach when examining the head, ears, nose, and throat region?","choices":["Rely on inspection alone, since palpation adds no useful information in this particular region","Move through the entire exam as quickly as possible, to minimize any patient discomfort","Avoid causing any discomfort under any circumstance, even if that limits exam completeness","Be thorough, systematic, and gentle; use look, smell, listen, and feel, without fearing necessary discomfort"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture's general approach emphasizes thoroughness, system, gentleness, using multiple senses, and not avoiding necessary discomfort. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"If the anorectal exam is being performed in conjunction with a pelvic exam on a female patient, the most practical combined positioning is...","choices":["standing bent at the waist","lithotomy position","left lateral position only, never combined with pelvic exam","prone with legs extended"],"answer":1,"exp":"When the anorectal exam accompanies a pelvic exam, the lithotomy position allows both exams to be performed efficiently in the same setup without needing to reposition the patient. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Acute epididymitis classically presents with...","choices":["a complete absence of any palpable abnormality despite significant active infection","a painless, firm nodule on the testis itself, developing gradually without tenderness","gradual onset of scrotal pain and tenderness localized to the epididymis, often with associated swelling","sudden onset of severe pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epididymitis typically presents with a more gradual onset of pain and tenderness localized to the epididymis (posterior to the testis), often with associated swelling and sometimes fever, in contrast to the sudden, severe pain of testicular torsion. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"In patients over 50 years old, assessing aortic width by pressing deeply with one hand on each side of the aorta, a normal aortic width should not exceed approximately...","choices":["0.5 cm","there is no defined normal width","3.0 cm","10.0 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"In patients over 50, aortic width is specifically assessed because of increased risk of aneurysm with age; a normal aorta should measure no more than about 3.0 cm wide, with wider measurements raising concern for an abdominal aortic aneurysm. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 54"}]};
